   E002 --> E003\n```\n\nConditional branches selected by the E001 outcome for HLR-1 (bounded, 3 outcomes):\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 Investigation\"]\n    BA[\"BRANCH-A-E004 Streamline config surface\"]\n    BB[\"BRANCH-B-E004 Fine-grained per-project controls\"]\n    BC[\"BRANCH-C-E004 Layered defaults + advanced controls\"]\n    E001 -. selects one .-> BA\n    E001 -. selects one .-> BB\n    E001 -. selects one .-> BC\n```\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 3, Rule 11 | The whitepaper's content (which usability practices to describe) is defined by the investigation outcomes; docs-authoring is sequenced after the available upstream work. Gate type: `rewrite` (outcome changes content, not existence) — recorded as `gated_by` on E003 |\n| E002 → E003 | Rule 3, Rule 11 | Onboarding findings are a primary input to the onboarding-practices section of the whitepaper |\n| E001 → BRANCH-A/B/C-E004 | Rule 3 | Bounded outcomes (≤3) → conditional decomposition branches. Each branch exists only under its outcome; gate type `remove` for every branch (mutually exclusive alternatives) |\n| E001 ∥ E002 | — | Independent unknowns resolved by different experiments (feedback consolidation vs onboarding current-state analysis); no edge |\n\n## Investigation Scoping Decisions\n\n- **Open question 1 (feedback precedence, HLR-1) and open question 3 (which enterprise\n  tools, HLR-3) are combined into E001**: both are resolved by the same experiment —\n  consolidating the multi-channel account feedback the problem statement says was never\n  consolidated — and produce the same deliverable (a consolidated feedback record plus a\n  decision record). Per the combination rule, they are not split.\n- **Open question 2 (onboarding scope, HLR-2) is E002**: a different experiment\n  (current-state journey analysis and scope definition) with a different deliverable.\n- **Decision rule applied**: all three answers