d via E001 with three conditional branches rather than\n   picking a winner during decomposition.\n3. **HLR-1/2/3 priorities are unspecified** (\"—\"). The gating investigations were\n   defaulted to P1 (they block all substantive work and leadership wants visible progress\n   this release); HLR-4 is P2 per the strategy. If product later rates any of HLR-1..3 as\n   P0, the gating investigation and selected branch must be raised to P0.\n4. **Phased delivery of implementation work**: no implementation epics exist yet for\n   HLR-1 (beyond the conditional branches), HLR-2, or HLR-3 because their scope is\n   genuinely unknown. A follow-up decomposition is required after E001/E002 resolve; the\n   strategy should not be treated as implementation-ready until then.\n5. **Scope trap — nothing is out of scope**: the strategy explicitly scopes nothing out\n   while its requirements are undefined; scope discipline must come from the investigation\n   decision records, not from this decomposition.\n6. **Whitepaper sequencing caveat (Rule 11)**: E003 is blocked by all *current* main-plan\n   Implementation epics (there are none besides itself). When the follow-up decomposition\n   creates implementation epics for HLR-1/2/3, those epics must be added as blockers of\n   the whitepaper so it describes what was actually built.\n7. **Priority inversion risk (non-blocking)**: HLR-4 (P2 whitepaper) is the only HLR with\n   an explicit priority while the substantive usability work is unprioritized — the\n   explicit P2 label on the least substantive deliverable is itself a signal the strategy\n   is under-refined.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable in the Rule 16-18 sense: no external dependency with uncertain timing\nexists. The related-but-different **phased** structure above applies: Phase 1 =\ninvestigations (E001, E002) plus the whitepaper (E003); Phase 2 = implementation epics\ndefined by the investigation outcomes (only the HLR-1 branches are pre-planned, because\nthat outcome space is bounded).\n\n## Conditional Branches\n\n| Branch | Outcome of E001 | Epic | Gate failure impact |\n|--------|----------------|------|---------------------|\n| A | Simplification takes precedence (\"fewer knobs\") | BRANCH-A-E004 | `remove` — epic exists only under outcome A |\n| B | Fine-grained per-project controls take precedence | BRANCH-B-E004 | `remove` — epic exists only under outcome B |\n| C | Layered resolution (curated defaults + opt-in advanced controls) | BRANCH-C-E004 | `remove` — epic exists only under outcome C |\n\nBranch epics carry `branch`, `gated_by=E001` (also in their `dependencies`), and\n`gate_failure_impact`. No main-plan epic depends on a branch epic; downstream work that\ndepends on the HLR-1 outcome depends on E001 itself.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":43085,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9eb016f57a34e3f4","agent_id":"a0cdb5112fc0036ce","session_id":"98e9d94a-7db9-4011-b4e9-ddd4548bd187","uuid":"11778db9-2f17-48ee-ad7f-6900e1a77960","timestamp":"2026-10-22T14:00:32.145Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9008"}
