{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b73eebdf1e32ecdf","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9008\n3\tepic_count: 3\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9008 (Make RHOAI more usable for enterprise teams)\n11\t\n12\t## Triage Outcome\n13\t\n14\tNeither Step 0 check fires; full decomposition was performed.\n15\t\n16\t- **Below-threshold**: does not apply. The strategy contains genuine unknowns — all three\n17\t  open questions (feedback precedence for HLR-1, onboarding scope for HLR-2, target tools\n18\t  for HLR-3) would change which epics exist or what they do. The scope also spans multiple\n19\t  distinct work streams (administration configuration, onboarding, enterprise integrations,\n20\t  and a whitepaper) across two components (Dashboard, Notebooks).\n21\t- **Docs-only**: does not apply. Dashboard and Notebooks are code components.\n22\t\n23\t## Step 1 — Parse Results and Missing Sections\n24\t\n25\tParsed: High-Level Requirements (HLR-1..4), Affected Components (Dashboard, Notebooks),\n26\tOut of Scope (nothing scoped out), Open Questions (3).\n27\t\n28\t**Missing strategy sections** (health warnings, decomposition proceeded with available data):\n29\tImpacted Teams table, Dependencies table, Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then),\n30\tNon-Functional Requirements, Staff Engineer Input, Technical Approach.\n31\t\n32\t## Step 1.5 — Staff Engineer Input\n33\t\n34\tNo Staff Engineer Input section exists in the strategy; no overrides to log.\n35\t\n36\t## Component Mapping\n37\t\n38\tBoth affected components (Dashboard, Notebooks) exist in\n39\t`.context/architecture-context/` (in platform, Konflux-onboarded, GA) — no onboarding\n40\tchain needed. Owning teams were taken from the architecture context because the strategy\n41\thas no Impacted Teams table:\n42\t\n43\t| Epic | Canonical component | Team | Note |\n44\t|------|--------------------|------|------|\n45\t| E001 | Dashboard | RHOAI Dashboard | Span: consolidation covers all feedback (Dashboard + Notebooks surfaces); Dashboard assigned as the contested HLR-1 administration surface (OdhDashboardConfig per dashboard.md) |\n46\t| E002 | Notebooks | Workbenches | Span: onboarding journey crosses Dashboard (project setup, spawn UI) and Notebooks (workbench); Notebooks assigned as the core data-scientist onboarding artifact |\n47\t| E003 | Documentation | RHOAI Documentation | Team assumed — no team information exists in the strategy |\n48\t| BRANCH-A/B/C-E004 | Dashboard | RHOAI Dashboard | HLR-1 implementation surface is the Dashboard administration configuration |\n49\t\n50\t## Epic List\n51\t\n52\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n53\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n54\t| E001 | Consolidate enterprise feedback and set the administration usability direction | Investigation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n55\t| E002 | Define data scientist onboarding scope and establish the current-state baseline | Investigation | Workbenches | P1 |\n56\t| E003 | Author and publish the RHOAI enterprise usability whitepaper | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | P2 |\n57\t| BRANCH-A-E004 | Streamline the administration configuration surface (conditional) | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n58\t| BRANCH-B-E004 | Add fine-grained per-project administration controls (conditional) | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n59\t| BRANCH-C-E004 | Implement layered administration defaults with opt-in advanced controls (conditional) | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n60\t\n61\t`epic_count` counts the 3 main-plan epics; the 3 branch epics are conditional plan\n62\tdocuments attached to E001, not standalone Jira issues.\n63\t\n64\t## Dependency DAG\n65\t\n66\tMain plan:\n67\t\n68\t```mermaid\n69\tgraph TD\n70\t    E001[\"E001 Consolidate feedback / set direction (Investigation)\"]\n71\t    E002[\"E002 Define onboarding scope + baseline (Investigation)\"]\n72\t    E003[\"E003 Enterprise usability whitepaper (docs-authoring)\"]\n73\t    E001 --> E003\n74\t    E002 --> E003\n75\t```\n76\t\n77\tConditional branches selected by the E001 outcome for HLR-1 (bounded, 3 outcomes):\n78\t\n79\t```mermaid\n80\tgraph TD\n81\t    E001[\"E001 Investigation\"]\n82\t    BA[\"BRANCH-A-E004 Streamline config surface\"]\n83\t    BB[\"BRANCH-B-E004 Fine-grained per-project controls\"]\n84\t    BC[\"BRANCH-C-E004 Layered defaults + advanced controls\"]\n85\t    E001 -. selects one .-> BA\n86\t    E001 -. selects one .-> BB\n87\t    E001 -. selects one .-> BC\n88\t```\n89\t\n90\t## DAG Justification\n91\t\n92\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n93\t|------|------|-----------|\n94\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3, Rule 11 | The whitepaper's content (which usability practices to describe) is defined by the investigation outcomes; docs-authoring is sequenced after the available upstream work. Gate type: `rewrite` (outcome changes content, not existence) — recorded as `gated_by` on E003 |\n95\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 3, Rule 11 | Onboarding findings are a primary input to the onboarding-practices section of the whitepaper |\n96\t| E001 → BRANCH-A/B/C-E004 | Rule 3 | Bounded outcomes (≤3) → conditional decomposition branches. Each branch exists only under its outcome; gate type `remove` for every branch (mutually exclusive alternatives) |\n97\t| E001 ∥ E002 | — | Independent unknowns resolved by different experiments (feedback consolidation vs onboarding current-state analysis); no edge |\n98\t\n99\t## Investigation Scoping Decisions\n100\t\n101\t- **Open question 1 (feedback precedence, HLR-1) and open question 3 (which enterprise\n102\t  tools, HLR-3) are combined into E001**: both are resolved by the same experiment —\n103\t  consolidating the multi-channel account feedback the problem statement says was never\n104\t  consolidated — and produce the same deliverable (a consolidated feedback record plus a\n105\t  decision record). Per the combination rule, they are not split.\n106\t- **Open question 2 (onboarding scope, HLR-2) is E002**: a different experiment\n107\t  (current-state journey analysis and scope definition) with a different deliverable.\n108\t- **Decision rule applied**: all three answers change which downstream epics exist or what\n109\t  they do → Investigation epics, not acceptance criteria.\n110\t- **HLR-1 outcome space is bounded** (simplify the configuration surface / add\n111\t  fine-grained per-project controls / layered combination of both) → conditional branch\n112\t  epics. **HLR-2 and HLR-3 downstream work is unbounded** (onboarding scope is any subset\n113\t  of cluster access, project setup, first workbench, first deployed model; integration\n114\t  targets are unnamed) → phased decomposition: implementation epics for HLR-2/HLR-3 are\n115\t  deliberately NOT invented now and must be created in a follow-up decomposition once the\n116\t  investigations resolve.\n117\t\n118\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n119\t\n120\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Coverage |\n121\t|-----|----------|---------|----------|\n122\t| HLR-1 | — (unspecified) | E001 (direction decision), BRANCH-A/B/C-E004 (conditional implementation) | The contradictory requirement (fewer knobs vs more fine-grained controls) is resolved by E001; the selected branch implements it |\n123\t| HLR-2 | — (unspecified) | E002 (scope definition + baseline) | Defines what onboarding covers and where the day is spent; implementation epics deferred to follow-up decomposition (phased) |\n124\t| HLR-3 | — (unspecified) | E001 (tool identification) | Names the concrete enterprise tools; implementation epics deferred to follow-up decomposition (phased) |\n125\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E003 | Direct coverage — whitepaper authored and published |\n126\t\n127\tNo HLR is orphaned. HLR-2/HLR-3 *implementation* work is intentionally gated behind the\n128\tinvestigations rather than speculatively scoped (see Health Warnings).\n129\t\n130\t## Health Warnings\n131\t\n132\t1. **Missing strategy sections**: no Impacted Teams, Dependencies, Acceptance Criteria,\n133\t   NFRs, or Staff Engineer Input. Teams were inferred from architecture context; the\n134\t   Documentation team for E003 is an assumption requiring confirmation.\n135\t2. **HLR-1 is self-contradictory** (\"fewer knobs\" vs \"more fine-grained controls\" from\n136\t   different accounts). Handled via E001 with three conditional branches rather than\n137\t   picking a winner during decomposition.\n138\t3. **HLR-1/2/3 priorities are unspecified** (\"—\"). The gating investigations were\n139\t   defaulted to P1 (they block all substantive work and leadership wants visible progress\n140\t   this release); HLR-4 is P2 per the strategy. If product later rates any of HLR-1..3 as\n141\t   P0, the gating investigation and selected branch must be raised to P0.\n142\t4. **Phased delivery of implementation work**: no implementation epics exist yet for\n143\t   HLR-1 (beyond the conditional branches), HLR-2, or HLR-3 because their scope is\n144\t   genuinely unknown. A follow-up decomposition is required after E001/E002 resolve; the\n145\t   strategy should not be treated as implementation-ready until then.\n146\t5. **Scope trap — nothing is out of scope**: the strategy explicitly scopes nothing out\n147\t   while its requirements are undefined; scope discipline must come from the investigation\n148\t   decision records, not from this decomposition.\n149\t6. **Whitepaper sequencing caveat (Rule 11)**: E003 is blocked by all *current* main-plan\n150\t   Implementation epics (there are none besides itself). When the follow-up decomposition\n151\t   creates implementation epics for HLR-1/2/3, those epics must be added as blockers of\n152\t   the whitepaper so it describes what was actually built.\n153\t7. **Priority inversion risk (non-blocking)**: HLR-4 (P2 whitepaper) is the only HLR with\n154\t   an explicit priority while the substantive usability work is unprioritized — the\n155\t   explicit P2 label on the least substantive deliverable is itself a signal the strategy\n156\t   is under-refined.\n157\t\n158\t## Tiered Delivery\n159\t\n160\tNot applicable in the Rule 16-18 sense: no external dependency with uncertain timing\n161\texists. The related-but-different **phased** structure above applies: Phase 1 =\n162\tinvestigations (E001, E002) plus the whitepaper (E003); Phase 2 = implementation epics\n163\tdefined by the investigation outcomes (only the HLR-1 branches are pre-planned, because\n164\tthat outcome space is bounded).\n165\t\n166\t## Conditional Branches\n167\t\n168\t| Branch | Outcome of E001 | Epic | Gate failure impact |\n169\t|--------|----------------|------|---------------------|\n170\t| A | Simplification takes precedence (\"fewer knobs\") | BRANCH-A-E004 | `remove` — epic exists only under outcome A |\n171\t| B | Fine-grained per-project controls take precedence | BRANCH-B-E004 | `remove` — epic exists only under outcome B |\n172\t| C | Layered resolution (curated defaults + opt-in advanced controls) | BRANCH-C-E004 | `remove` — epic exists only under outcome C |\n173\t\n174\tBranch epics carry `branch`, `gated_by=E001` (also in their `dependencies`), and\n175\t`gate_failure_impact`. No main-plan epic depends on a branch epic; downstream work that\n176\tdepends on the HLR-1 outcome depends on E001 itself.\n177\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8cf1aa6872d45160","agent_id":"a705b96d1401caf34","session_id":"98e9d94a-7db9-4011-b4e9-ddd4548bd187","uuid":"d11d5fd4-45f8-4751-9c43-894be62b57ea","timestamp":"2026-10-22T14:06:13.001Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b73eebdf1e32ecdf","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9008 decomposition"}
