resent; no HLR orphaned. HLR-1 → E001 + BRANCH-A/B/C-E004; HLR-2 →\n41\tE002; HLR-3 → E001 (tool identification); HLR-4 → E003. No P0/P1 HLRs exist in the\n42\tstrategy (HLR-1..3 are \"—\", HLR-4 is P2); defaulting the gating investigations to P1 is\n43\tdocumented with rationale (Health Warning 3). E003's P2 is correct for a docs-authoring\n44\tepic under a Normal-priority strategy. E001 combining HLR-1 and HLR-3 is not priority\n45\tcollapse: both are unspecified-priority open questions resolved by the same experiment\n46\t(feedback consolidation), which the combination rule explicitly permits. The deferred\n47\tHLR-2/HLR-3 implementation work is explicitly justified (phased decomposition, Health\n48\tWarning 4), not silently dropped.\n49\t\n50\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n51\tNo cycles. Diagram edges (E001→E003, E002→E003) exactly match E003's frontmatter\n52\t`dependencies: [E001, E002]`; branch diagrams match branch frontmatter (`gated_by`/deps =\n53\tE001). Arrow direction follows the dependency→dependent convention. E001 ∥ E002 are\n54\tcorrectly not serialized (independent unknowns, different experiments). All three branch\n55\tfiles set both `branch` (A/B/C, matching filenames) and `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-9008-E001`,\n56\twith the gate target in direct `dependencies`. No main-plan epic depends on a branch epic.\n57\tNo konflux/license-validation epics exist, so no implementation-type parallelism rules\n58\tapply; no edge is justified by an image digest or endpoint reference. Critical path\n59\tlength 2 is appropriate for the strategy size. Minor observation (see issues): the\n60\tselected BRANCH-E004 implementation does not block E003; the summary addresses this via\n61\tHealth Warning 6 and the branches' mutually-exclusive conditional nature (an edge from a\n62\tnon-selected branch cannot exist), and the validator's Rule 11 check passes.\n63\t\n64\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n65\tEach epic is a single component/team tuple (Dashboard/RHOAI Dashboard,\n66\tNotebooks/Workbenches, Documentation/RHOAI Documentation); teams verified against the\n67\tarchitecture context, and the assumed Documentation team is flagged in Health Warning 1.\n68\tNo epic is oversized — all are investigation/authoring/single-surface work well under two\n69\tweeks. No scope duplication: the three branches are mutually exclusive alternatives\n70\tselected by E001's outcome, not independent builds of the same surface; E001 and E002 are\n71\tdifferent experiments with different deliverables. No work-product mixing: E003 is pure\n72\tcontent authoring; BRANCH-A's caption/doc updates are incidental to the code change and\n73\texplicitly exclude new content authoring.\n74\t\n75\t### 4. Type Correctness (1/2)\n76\tTypes are correct: E001 and E002 are genuine Investigations (their outcomes select the\n77\tbranch, name the integration targets, and define the onboarding scope for the follow-up\n78\tdecomposition — downstream structure genuinely changes); E003 and the branches are\n79\tImplementations producing concrete artifacts. All `gated_by` targets appear in direct\n80\t`dependencies`, and every non-null `gated_by` has a populated `gate_failure_impact`\n81\t(action + fallback_approach). One gating-metadata gap: E003 lists two Investigations in\n82\t`dependencies` (E001, E002) but `gated_by` records only E001. E002's outcome (onboarding\n83\tdefinition and baseline) rewrites a primary section of the whitepaper — the same\n84\t`rewrite` character as E001 — yet the gate and the `gate_failure_impact` fallback mention\n85\tonly the feedback investigation. This is the rubric's level-1 case (\"an Investigation\n86\tdependency missing `gated_by` on a downstream epic\"); note the schema's `gated_by` is a\n87\tsingle string, so only one of the two gates can be recorded, and the decomposer chose the\n88\tprimary one. Deducted one point accordingly.\n89\t\n90\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n91\tType-appropriate signal sets throughout: E001/E002 carry `investigation_signals`\n92\t(5-signal rubric); E003 and all branches carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric); no epic\n93\tmixes the sets. Rationales in the ai-signals files match the frontmatter values and are\n94\tgrounded in verifiable architecture context (OdhDashboardConfig per-component feature\n95\tflags in dashboard.md; per-workbench culling overrides via OdhDashboardConfig\n96\tnotebookController settings in notebooks.md; owning teams RHOAI Dashboard and Workbenches\n97\t— all confirmed). E001's `source_accessibility: 0` is correct (answers live in\n98\tunconsolidated feedback channels and stakeholder decisions, not readable artifacts), and\n99\t`human_judgment_required: -2` is justified (precedence policy and tool selection are\n100\tproduct decisions). E002's `source_accessibility: 1` is borderline but defensible: the\n101\trationale correctly partitions the source-accessible technical substance (current-state\n102\tpath from docs and component source) from the human ratification captured under\n103\t`human_judgment_required: -1`. Branch signal differentiation is sound (BRANCH-B\n104\t`change_specificity: -1` / `existing_foundation: 0` for the genuinely new per-project\n105\tcontrol level, vs BRANCH-A/C building on the documented surface). Arithmetic/thresholds\n106\tnot checked per instructions.\n107\t\n108\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n109\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy. Rule-mandated ACs present: E003\n110\t(docs-authoring) has the technical-review-for-accuracy AC; BRANCH-A and BRANCH-C, which\n111\tmodify an existing capability, both have reversibility/feature-flag ACs; BRANCH-B is\n112\tadditive and instead carries default-preservation and regression-test ACs, which covers\n113\tthe same safety concern. No AC states an external schedule constraint (no lab turnaround\n114\tor review-board cadence misfiled as an AC). No epic body references sibling epics by\n115\tdraft ID — upstream work is referenced by role (\"the investigation decision record\"),\n116\twhich survives Jira creation.\n117\t\n118\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n119\tAll strategy scope is accounted for: all four HLRs covered, all three open questions\n120\tmapped to investigations (Q1+Q3 → E001 per the same-experiment combination rule, Q2 →\n121\tE002), and both affected components (Dashboard, Notebooks) appear in the epic set. The\n122\tconditional branches cover the full bounded HLR-1 outcome space (simplification /\n123\tfine-grained / layered), made exhaustive by E001's AC requiring exactly one selection.\n124\tThe HLR-1 contradiction, unspecified priorities, missing strategy sections, and the\n125\t\"nothing is out of scope\" trap are all carried forward as health warnings rather than\n126\tsilently dropped. The docs-authoring requirement (HLR-4) has its epic, correctly\n127\tsequenced after the available upstream work with the Rule 11 future-blocker caveat\n128\tdocumented (Health Warning 6).\n129\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8b305912d97c92d7","agent_id":"a6d2ecbd0d01bdd6b","session_id":"98e9d94a-7db9-4011-b4e9-ddd4548bd187","uuid":"e03fcbbc-d59a-4b94-a3ff-4c38ad94256b","timestamp":"2026-10-22T14:14:47.367Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b2f6568273cd94e2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9008 decomposition"}
