 scoped (see Health Warnings).\n128\t\n129\t## Health Warnings\n130\t\n131\t1. **Missing strategy sections**: no Impacted Teams, Dependencies, Acceptance Criteria,\n132\t   NFRs, or Staff Engineer Input. Teams were inferred from architecture context; the\n133\t   Documentation team for E003 is an assumption requiring confirmation.\n134\t2. **HLR-1 is self-contradictory** (\"fewer knobs\" vs \"more fine-grained controls\" from\n135\t   different accounts). Handled via E001 with three conditional branches rather than\n136\t   picking a winner during decomposition.\n137\t3. **HLR-1/2/3 priorities are unspecified** (\"—\"). The gating investigations were\n138\t   defaulted to P1 (they block all substantive work and leadership wants visible progress\n139\t   this release); HLR-4 is P2 per the strategy. If product later rates any of HLR-1..3 as\n140\t   P0, the gating investigation and selected branch must be raised to P0.\n141\t4. **Phased delivery of implementation work**: no implementation epics exist yet for\n142\t   HLR-1 (beyond the conditional branches), HLR-2, or HLR-3 because their scope is\n143\t   genuinely unknown. A follow-up decomposition is required after E001/E002 resolve; the\n144\t   strategy should not be treated as implementation-ready until then.\n145\t5. **Scope trap — nothing is out of scope**: the strategy explicitly scopes nothing out\n146\t   while its requirements are undefined; scope discipline must come from the investigation\n147\t   decision records, not from this decomposition.\n148\t6. **Whitepaper sequencing caveat (Rule 11)**: E003 is blocked by all *current* main-plan\n149\t   Implementation epics (there are none besides itself). When the follow-up decomposition\n150\t   creates implementation epics for HLR-1/2/3, those epics must be added as blockers of\n151\t   the whitepaper so it describes what was actually built.\n152\t7. **Priority inversion risk (non-blocking)**: HLR-4 (P2 whitepaper) is the only HLR with\n153\t   an explicit priority while the substantive usability work is unprioritized — the\n154\t   explicit P2 label on the least substantive deliverable is itself a signal the strategy\n155\t   is under-refined.\n156\t\n157\t## Tiered Delivery\n158\t\n159\tNot applicable in the Rule 16-18 sense: no external dependency with uncertain timing\n160\texists. The related-but-different **phased** structure above applies: Phase 1 =\n161\tinvestigations (E001, E002) plus the whitepaper (E003); Phase 2 = implementation epics\n162\tdefined by the investigation outcomes (only the HLR-1 branches are pre-planned, because\n163\tthat outcome space is bounded).\n164\t\n165\t## Conditional Branches\n166\t\n167\t| Branch | Outcome of E001 | Epic | Gate failure impact |\n168\t|--------|----------------|------|---------------------|\n169\t| A | Simplification takes precedence (\"fewer knobs\") | BRANCH-A-E004 | `remove` — epic exists only under outcome A |\n170\t| B | Fine-grained per-project controls take precedence | BRANCH-B-E004 | `remove` — epic exists only under outcome B |\n171\t| C | Layered resolution (curated defaults + opt-in advanced controls) | BRANCH-C-E004 | `remove` — epic exists only under outcome C |\n172\t\n173\tBranch epics carry `branch`, `gated_by=E001` (also in their `dependencies`), and\n174\t`gate_failure_impact`. No main-plan epic depends on a branch epic; downstream work that\n175\tdepends on the HLR-1 outcome depends on E001 itself.\n176\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8b305912d97c92d7","agent_id":"a6d2ecbd0d01bdd6b","session_id":"98e9d94a-7db9-4011-b4e9-ddd4548bd187","uuid":"913f3a22-f9e6-40d7-b4a7-6bc89bc2035a","timestamp":"2026-10-22T14:15:03.813Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a284cde6b314e017","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9008 decomposition"}
