on (phased) |\n125\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E003 | Direct coverage — whitepaper authored and published |\n126\t\n127\tNo HLR is orphaned. HLR-2/HLR-3 *implementation* work is intentionally gated behind the\n128\tinvestigations rather than speculatively scoped (see Health Warnings).\n129\t\n130\t## Health Warnings\n131\t\n132\t1. **Missing strategy sections**: no Impacted Teams, Dependencies, Acceptance Criteria,\n133\t   NFRs, or Staff Engineer Input. Teams were inferred from architecture context; the\n134\t   Documentation team for E003 is an assumption requiring confirmation.\n135\t2. **HLR-1 is self-contradictory** (\"fewer knobs\" vs \"more fine-grained controls\" from\n136\t   different accounts). Handled via E001 with three conditional branches rather than\n137\t   picking a winner during decomposition.\n138\t3. **HLR-1/2/3 priorities are unspecified** (\"—\"). The gating investigations were\n139\t   defaulted to P1 (they block all substantive work and leadership wants visible progress\n140\t   this release); HLR-4 is P2 per the strategy. If product later rates any of HLR-1..3 as\n141\t   P0, the gating investigation and selected branch must be raised to P0.\n142\t4. **Phased delivery of implementation work**: no implementation epics exist yet for\n143\t   HLR-1 (beyond the conditional branches), HLR-2, or HLR-3 because their scope is\n144\t   genuinely unknown. A follow-up decomposition is required after E001/E002 resolve; the\n145\t   strategy should not be treated as implementation-ready until then.\n146\t5. **Scope trap — nothing is out of scope**: the strategy explicitly scopes nothing out\n147\t   while its requirements are undefined; scope discipline must come from the investigation\n148\t   decision records, not from this decomposition.\n149\t6. **Whitepaper sequencing caveat (Rule 11)**: E003 is blocked by all *current* main-plan\n150\t   Implementation epics (there are none besides itself). The branch epics are conditional\n151\t   alternatives, so no fixed edge from them can exist yet — but **when E001 selects a\n152\t   branch, that branch epic is promoted into the main plan as the HLR-1 Implementation\n153\t   epic and must immediately be added as a blocker of E003** so the whitepaper describes\n154\t   what was actually built. The same applies when the follow-up decomposition creates\n155\t   implementation epics for HLR-2/HLR-3: those epics must be added as blockers of the\n156\t   whitepaper.\n157\t7. **Priority inversion risk (non-blocking)**: HLR-4 (P2 whitepaper) is the only HLR with\n158\t   an explicit priority while the substantive usability work is unprioritized — the\n159\t   explicit P2 label on the least substantive deliverable is itself a signal the strategy\n160\t   is under-refined.\n161\t\n162\t## Tiered Delivery\n163\t\n164\tNot applicable in the Rule 16-18 sense: no external dependency with uncertain timing\n165\texists. The related-but-different **phased** structure above applies: Phase 1 =\n166\tinvestigations (E001, E002) plus the whitepaper (E003); Phase 2 = implementation epics\n167\tdefined by the investigation outcomes (only the HLR-1 branches are pre-planned, because\n168\tthat outcome space is bounded).\n169\t\n170\t## Conditional Branches\n171\t\n172\t| Branch | Outcome of E001 | Epic | Gate failure impact |\n173\t|--------|----------------|------|---------------------|\n174\t| A | Simplification takes precedence (\"fewer knobs\") | BRANCH-A-E004 | `remove` — epic exists only under outcome A |\n175\t| B | Fine-grained per-project controls take precedence | BRANCH-B-E004 | `remove` — epic exists only under outcome B |\n176\t| C | Layered resolution (curated defaults + opt-in advanced controls) | BRANCH-C-E004 | `remove` — epic exists only under outcome C |\n177\t\n178\tBranch epics carry `branch`, `gated_by=E001` (also in their `dependencies`), and\n179\t`gate_failure_impact`. No main-plan epic depends on a branch epic; downstream work that\n180\tdepends on the HLR-1 outcome depends on E001 itself. **On selection** (E001's decision\n181\trecord names exactly one outcome): the chosen branch epic is promoted into the main plan\n182\tand, per Rule 11, must be added as a blocker of the docs-authoring epic before the\n183\twhitepaper is drafted; the two unselected branch files are discarded.\n184\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b0e45f1a5d9d5b82","agent_id":"a00317b63ea50ba62","session_id":"98e9d94a-7db9-4011-b4e9-ddd4548bd187","uuid":"0848cb87-013c-43b1-930f-efabc440bd10","timestamp":"2026-10-22T14:18:12.851Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-99dfa7322412371b","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9008"}
