d for technical accuracy against the RHOAI\n47\tarchitecture context.\n48\t\n49\tWhen the administration-direction investigation selects an implementation approach for the\n50\tadministration configuration surface, that selected implementation becomes a blocker of\n51\tthis epic: it must be added to this epic's dependencies before drafting begins, so the\n52\twhitepaper describes the practices that were actually built rather than a plan that\n53\tchanged after drafting.\n54\t\n55\t## Scope\n56\t\n57\t- Content outline for the whitepaper covering enterprise usability best practices on\n58\t  RHOAI across three areas: administration and configuration practices, data scientist\n59\t  onboarding practices, and integration with enterprise tooling.\n60\t- Draft the whitepaper through the Red Hat docs content process, grounded in the\n61\t  investigation decision records (consolidated feedback, selected administration\n62\t  direction, named integration targets, onboarding definition and baseline).\n63\t- Validate the draft for technical accuracy against the RHOAI architecture context for\n64\t  the affected components (Dashboard, Notebooks) and the investigation findings.\n65\t- Publish the whitepaper through the designated Red Hat content channel.\n66\t\n67\t## Acceptance Criteria\n68\t\n69\t- A content outline exists covering administration, onboarding, and enterprise\n70\t  integration practices, and is agreed with the strategy owner.\n71\t- The draft is technically reviewed for accuracy against the investigation decision\n72\t  records and the RHOAI architecture context (Dashboard and Notebooks component\n73\t  documentation); every product claim in the draft traces to a reviewed source.\n74\t- The whitepaper is published in the designated Red Hat content channel.\n75\t\n76\t## HLR Traceability\n77\t\n78\t- **HLR-4** (publish a whitepaper describing enterprise usability best practices on\n79\t  RHOAI): direct, complete coverage.\n80\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmp